content.thinking:
OK so `finishSequenceAndLogError` calls `finishSequence()` internally. In the original code:
```java
case (byte) '\\': {
    String dcs = ...;
    if (dcs.startsWith("$q")) {
        if (dcs.equals("$q\"p")) {
            ...
        } else {
            finishSequenceAndLogError("Unrecognized DECRQSS string: '" + dcs + "'");
        }
    } else if (dcs.startsWith("+q")) {
        ...
    } else {
        ...
    }
    finishSequence();   // ← This ALWAYS runs after the if-else chain
}
```

So when we call `finishSequenceAndLogError`, it invokes `finishSequence()` internally, and then `finishSequence()` runs again at the end of the case block. Since `finishSequence()` just resets the escape state, calling it twice is harmless and idempotent, so extracting the error handling into a separate method won't cause any issues.

For the `doDeviceControlRequestTermcap` method, I need to extract the complex loop that processes termcap entries and the hex encoding logic that follows it. Writing termcap request handler... Handling unrecognized termcap codes by logging warnings and sending invalid responses, then encoding the response values as hexadecimal strings for transmission back to the session.

Now I'm refactoring the device control handler to delegate to specialized methods—extracting the device control string and routing it to either the status string handler or termcap handler based on its prefix, which cuts down the main method from 120 lines to about 31. Refactoring the case handler to extract the logic into separate methods...

